
Soft Link (Symbolic Link) : A soft link is like a shortcut to another file.
=========================

# Create symlink for file
ln -s original.txt softlink.txt


# create symlink for directory
ln -s directory  directory


# to unlink
unlink file1   --> removes the directory entry (name) file1. Whether the actual data becomes inaccessible/deleted depends on whether other links to the same inode exist.



# Example:
original.txt
     ↑
     |
softlink.txt


# If you run: cat softlink.txt
Linux follows the link and reads original.txt.


readlink -f symlink_file    --> to know physical path of file


ln -s /home/viswa/file1.txt(target file)  /tmp/file2.txt(link)
ls -l /tmp/file2.txt   --> You should see something like:  lrwxrwxrwx. 1 viswa viswa 21 Oct 4 16:52 /tmp/file2.txt -> /home/viswa/file1.txt  --> it is link details
ls -lL /tmp/file2.txt  --> You should get something like:  -rw-r--r--. 1 viswa viswa 8 Oct 4 16:53 /tmp/file2.txt                            --> it is target details


# You might see:
-rw-r--r-- 1 user user 100 Aug 16 original.txt
lrwxrwxrwx 1 user user  12 Aug 16 softlink.txt -> original.txt

The l at the beginning means it is a symbolic link.


# To see the physical path of directory
pwd -P

pwd -P  --> to know physical directory for symbolic link
cd -P /home/viswa/logs     --> to change directory of physical directory of path /home/viswa/logs
cd -L /home/viswa/logs     --> to preserve directory Logical path


Important characteristics
=========================
Can link to a file or directory
Can point to a file on a different filesystem
Has its own inode
If the original file is deleted, the soft link becomes broken
Can point to another soft link

